WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.460
Somewhere beneath the western Pacific,

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:04.960
there is a place that sunlight has never touched.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.860
It lies at the southern end of the Mariana Trench,

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.600
almost eleven kilometres down.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.540
This is the Challenger Deep,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.900
the deepest known point in Earth's ocean.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.390
Here, the water presses down with more than a thousand times the

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.440
pressure at the surface.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:19.430
It is cold.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.140
And it is completely dark.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.760
So what could possibly live here?

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.310
The truth is stranger, and more fragile,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.020
than any monster we could invent.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.780
Our descent begins in sunlit water.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.680
Near the surface, there is enough light for photosynthesis,

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.460
the energy source behind most of the ocean's food webs.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.450
By two hundred metres,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.300
so little light remains that photosynthesis is no longer possible.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.620
Light can still be detected as far as a thousand metres down,

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:49.540
but it is faint.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.960
Below one thousand metres, sunlight does not reach at all.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.500
At four thousand metres we enter the abyss.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.800
At six thousand, the seafloor drops away into the trenches.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.840
This is the hadal zone, named after Hades,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.060
the Greek god of the underworld.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:05.760
These trenches are rare.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.690
The forty-six known hadal habitats cover less than a quarter

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.480
of one percent of the seafloor,

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.480
yet they make up over forty percent of the ocean's depth range.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.810
At the very bottom of the deepest of them,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.210
we reach the Challenger Deep:

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.150
about ten thousand, nine hundred and thirty-five metres.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.450
If Mount Everest were placed here,

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.860
its summit would still be more than two kilometres underwater.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.770
Every ten metres adds roughly one more atmosphere of pressure.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.020
At the bottom,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.420
that comes to more than one thousand atmospheres,

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.400
about sixteen thousand pounds on every square inch.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.340
The temperature is just one to four degrees Celsius.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.270
Back above us, in the twilight zone,

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.140
the last traces of sunlight still reach the animals that live there.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.500
Further down, in total darkness,

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.550
the only light is the light that animals make themselves.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.130
Bioluminescence is a chemical reaction,

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.000
and it is widespread among deep-sea animals.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.300
These animals live in different depth zones,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.470
thousands of metres above the Challenger Deep.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.670
As we go deeper, the cast of characters changes.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.810
In the trench, there are no plants.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.130
So where does the energy come from?

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:11.510
Mostly, from above. Dead plankton,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.500
fragments of animals and other organic matter sink slowly

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:15.390
through the water,

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.550
in what scientists call marine snow.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.310
Much of it is eaten or broken down before it arrives,

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.110
but trenches act like traps.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:24.810
In the Challenger Deep,

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.880
scientists measured oxygen use by microbes in the sediment

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.790
at twice the rate of a nearby site six thousand metres deep.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:33.690
Earthquakes may help.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.900
They can shake material loose from the trench walls and send

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.130
it sliding to the bottom.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.190
Even sunken plant material may be food.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.560
Amphipods caught at nearly eleven thousand metres carry

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.010
enzymes that can digest cellulose,

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.310
the main material in wood and plant fibres.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:50.660
Elsewhere,

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.670
some seafloor communities run on chemistry instead of light.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:55.190
Around hot vents,

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.770
bacteria turn chemicals like hydrogen sulphide into energy.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.230
These vents are a different environment,

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.770
but they show that life does not always need the sun.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.990
So, who actually lives at the bottom?

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.910
Here is what has been documented.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:13.180
Amphipods. Small, shrimp-like crustaceans. In 2009,

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.320
Japanese researchers lowered a baited trap into the

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.490
Challenger Deep and brought up one hundred and eighty-five of them.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.470
The species is called Hirondellea gigas.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:24.400
Single-celled giants called xenophyophores.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.420
Some can grow as large as a saucer.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.920
They have been seen on the floor of the Challenger Deep,

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.440
and related single-celled organisms have been found at

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.690
around ten thousand six hundred metres.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.790
Sea cucumbers. In 2012,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.420
camera footage from the Challenger Deep showed a small grey

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.250
sea cucumber, unlike any scientists had seen.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.480
Several of them faced the same direction,

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.310
as if letting the current carry food to their tentacles.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.390
Worms. In 2009,

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:54.950
the robot Nereus spotted a polychaete worm about an inch long.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:56.430
Years before that,

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:59.070
Japan's Kaiko robot had recorded a sea cucumber,

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.510
a scale worm and a shrimp.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.270
And fish? Not at the very bottom.

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:08.630
The deepest fish ever documented live thousands of metres higher up.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.660
The Mariana snailfish is known from about six thousand to

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:13.670
eight thousand metres.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:15.700
It is pale, tadpole-like,

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.910
and reaches about twenty-nine centimetres.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.550
In parts of the trench, it appears to be the top predator,

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.630
feeding on small crustaceans.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:28.260
In 1960, the crew of the Trieste reported seeing a flatfish on the

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:29.790
bottom. Today,

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:33.470
some scientists suggest it may have been a sea cucumber instead.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:36.290
Science does not always settle things quickly.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.110
Beyond animals, there are microbes.

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:41.530
They are abundant in trench sediments,

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.850
and they may be the base of the whole system.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.850
How do animals cope with this much pressure?

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:49.270
Mostly, by not fighting it.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.400
Animals without gas-filled spaces have pressure on the

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.590
inside equal to the pressure outside.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:56.870
The challenge is chemistry.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.400
Pressure bends proteins out of shape.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.230
Many deep-sea animals counter it with small molecules,

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.430
such as trimethylamine N-oxide,

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:07.310
that help proteins keep their form.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.860
In fish, the amount of that molecule rises with depth.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:14.640
Measurements on snailfish from seven thousand metres suggest

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:19.220
a limit. Around eight thousand two hundred metres,

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:22.310
a fish's body fluids would match the saltiness of seawater.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:25.360
That is a hypothesis, supported by evidence,

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:27.350
and not an unbreakable law.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:30.570
Fish have been filmed deeper than eight thousand metres.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.450
But as far as published research shows,

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.610
no fish have been found in the deepest quarter of the ocean.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:38.430
Other tricks help,

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.780
like slower metabolisms and cell membranes built to stay

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:43.030
flexible under pressure.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:44.470
In the lab,

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.570
the amphipod's enzyme even works better at high pressure.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.530
The Challenger Deep is named after the ship HMS Challenger,

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:56.830
whose crew first sounded the trench in 1875. In 1951,

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:00.060
a second ship named Challenger confirmed its great depth

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.070
using sonar.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.340
On the twenty-third of January 1960, two men,

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:06.870
Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh,

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.010
descended in the bathyscaphe Trieste.

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:11.590
The trip down took almost five hours.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:15.000
They spent about twenty minutes on the bottom,

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.770
then began the three-hour climb home.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.810
Robots followed. Japan's Kaiko in 1995.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:24.130
The hybrid vehicle Nereus in 2009.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.900
In 2012, filmmaker James Cameron made the first solo dive,

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:28.660
in Deepsea Challenger.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:32.690
It reached ten thousand, nine hundred and eight metres.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:37.120
From 2019, the submersible Limiting Factor made repeated dives.

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:38.700
In June 2020,

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:42.000
former astronaut Kathy Sullivan became the first woman to

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:42.940
reach the Challenger Deep.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:44.540
In November that year,

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:47.680
China's Fendouzhe took three scientists to about ten thousand,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.330
nine hundred and nine metres.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:55.290
By mid-2022, twenty-seven people had reached the Challenger Deep.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:57.180
Even the depth is not settled.

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.100
The best estimate is ten thousand,

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:03.500
nine hundred and thirty-five metres, give or take six.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:05.520
Different surveys give slightly different answers,

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.160
and measuring through cold, salty,

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.110
pressurised water is hard.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:11.480
As of April 2026,

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:14.720
only about twenty-nine percent of the global seafloor has

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.890
been mapped in modern high resolution.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:21.280
And explorers have directly seen less than one thousandth of

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.290
one percent of the deep seafloor.

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.650
That is roughly the area of Rhode Island.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.380
More than four hundred species are known from hadal ecosystems,

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:32.070
and new ones are still being found.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:34.570
Others, like that grey sea cucumber,

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:36.890
cannot be confirmed without physical samples.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:39.500
How do living things cope at the extremes,

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:41.530
and how far down can animals go?

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:43.650
Those questions are still open.

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:48.990
We return now to the darkness. No sunlight. Crushing pressure.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.110
Almost no one has been here.

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.710
The deepest place on Earth is not empty.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:56.750
It is a world we have only just begun to see.
